course: everyday
lesson: 68
title: Chickens, goats and little creatures
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every animal, big or small, has its own way of life, and a kind person learns it: chickens need to be shut in safe at night, goats explore with their mouths, and insects have fragile bodies that are easily hurt.
explain: Small creatures are not toys. Butterflies lose the tiny scales on their wings when touched, fireflies and other insects die quickly in jars, and every little animal has a job in nature.
explain: Ladybirds, called ladybugs in North America, eat greenfly (aphids) that damage plants, and earthworms keep soil healthy, so gardeners are glad to see them.
explain: Some small creatures need care from us: the hairs of some caterpillars cause rashes, and head lice spread from head to head, which is common and not a sign of being dirty.
explain: After a rainy night a boy in Ireland finds worms drying out on the path to school. He gently moves a few back onto the grass, because worms that stay on the hard path can dry out and die.
explain: Children in Ohio cuddled and kissed backyard chickens; several got Salmonella and were sick for days.
explain: A girl in England turned her back on a billy goat at a farm; it butted her from behind and knocked her down into the mud, bruising her back.
fact: Backyard chickens (called chooks in Australia) are shut in at night because foxes and other predators attack in the dark.
fact: Chickens can carry salmonella germs, so you wash your hands after handling them or their eggs.
fact: Goats explore with their mouths and chew almost anything, so you keep loose things out of their reach.
fact: Some roosters protect their hens by attacking, so you walk away calmly and tell the owner.
fact: Butterfly wings are fragile and covered with tiny scales that rub off when touched.
fact: Insects die quickly in jars, so a bug is kept only for a short time with air, leaves and damp, then released where it was found.
fact: Ants come into kitchens for food, so you clean up crumbs and sticky spills.
fact: Every small creature has a job in nature, and hurting them for fun is unkind.
fact: Head lice (people often call them nits, the name of their eggs) spread by head-to-head contact and are common, not a sign of being dirty.
fact: Ladybirds eat greenfly and help gardens.
fact: The hairs of some caterpillars, such as the oak processionary, cause rashes, so you do not touch hairy caterpillars.
fact: Fireflies glow to find each other.
fact: Worms come up when the ground is wet and can dry out on paths; they keep soil healthy.
fact: A worm cut in half does not become two worms; usually only one part survives, if any.
fact: Backyard chickens and ducks often carry Salmonella, even when they look clean and healthy.
fact: Goats can butt hard enough to knock a child over; a goat that lowers its head may be about to butt, so you move away calmly and never turn your back on it.
fact: Some frogs and toads have poison on their skin and can carry germs, so you wash your hands after touching them.
exercise: Why are backyard chickens shut in their coop at night? | Foxes and other predators attack at night | Chickens are afraid of the moon and the stars | The eggs would freeze outside | Chickens cannot see their food or water in the dark
exercise: What should you do after collecting eggs from your chickens? | Wash your hands | Lick your fingers clean | Nothing, eggs are always clean | Wipe your hands on the hens
exercise: A goat at the petting zoo starts nibbling your jacket. What should you do? | Step away and keep loose things out of its reach | Let it eat the jacket, because it is clearly very hungry | Hit it on the nose | Feed it your scarf instead
exercise: A rooster runs at you in a farmyard. What should you do? | Walk away calmly and tell the owner | Chase it back round the yard to show who is boss | Kick it to make it stop | Pick it up and hold it tight
exercise: You want to see a butterfly up close. What is best? | Watch it, or catch it gently in a net and let it go at once | Hold it firmly by both wings so it cannot fly away from you | Keep it in your pocket | Rub its wings to see the colours
exercise: Why should you not hold a butterfly by its wings? | Its wings are fragile and their tiny scales rub off | The wings are poisonous to touch | It will bite your fingers hard if you hold it like that | The wings will stick to your skin
exercise: You catch a beetle to look at. What is the kind way to keep it? | Briefly, with air, leaves and damp, then let it go where you found it | In a closed jar on the sunny windowsill for weeks, so you can watch it every day | In a box with no air holes | In the freezer to keep it fresh
exercise: Ants are marching into your kitchen. What should you do? | Clean up crumbs and spills and tell an adult | Leave food out on the floor so they all go away full | Pour hot water on everyone's feet | Put sugar by the door
exercise: Some children are stepping on ants for fun. What is right? | Leave the ants alone | Join in, ants do not matter | Use a magnifying glass on them | Count how many they squash
exercise: Your head is very itchy and a friend says you might have lice. What should you do? | Tell an adult and do not share hats and combs | Hide it from everyone, because lice mean you are dirty | Share your comb so the lice spread out | Cut all your hair off yourself
exercise: A ladybird lands on your hand in the garden. What should you do? | Let it walk and fly away | Squash it before it bites | Put it in a jar for a week | Brush it into the pond
exercise: Why do gardeners like ladybirds? | Ladybirds eat greenfly that harm plants | Ladybirds water the plants | Ladybirds make the soil softer and richer for roots | Ladybirds scare birds away
exercise: You see a fuzzy, hairy caterpillar on a tree. What should you do? | Not touch it, some hairs cause rashes | Stroke it, because hairy ones are always friendly | Put it down your friend's shirt | Hold it on your cheek
exercise: On a summer evening fireflies are glowing. What should you do? | Watch them but not keep them in a jar | Catch as many as you can in a jar to take home | Squash them to see the light | Catch one to use as a night light
exercise: You see a worm on the hot path after rain. What is kind? | Gently move it to soil or grass | Leave it there in the sun to dry off | Cut it in half to make two | Put it in your lunchbox
exercise: Does a worm cut in half become two worms? | No, usually only one part survives, if any | Yes, both halves always grow into new worms | Yes, but only in summer | Yes, if you put them in water
test: A family in Kent forgot to shut their chickens in for the night. In the morning some hens are missing. What most likely happened? | A fox or other predator attacked in the dark | The hens flew south for the winter | The hens went off to lay their eggs somewhere else | The hens were too warm and left
test: A girl in Ohio wants to keep a firefly in a jar as a pet lamp. Why is this a bad idea? | Fireflies die in jars, and they glow to find each other | Fireflies are far too bright to keep inside a bedroom | Fireflies only glow in the daytime | Fireflies eat the jar lid
test: Lice are found in a class at school. A boy says only dirty children get lice. Which is true? | Lice are common and spread by head-to-head contact, not dirt | Only children who never wash their hair or have baths get lice | Lice come from eating sweets | Lice live in school desks
test: A boy brings a caterpillar to school and keeps it in a closed jar with no leaves for a week. What will probably happen? | It will die, because insects need air, food and damp | It will turn into a butterfly much faster than outside | It will grow bigger in the dark | Nothing, caterpillars need nothing
test: Why do worms come up onto paths after heavy rain? | The ground is wet, and they can then dry out on paths | They want to sunbathe | They are looking for a new garden to live in for good | Rain makes them hungry for stones
test: A girl's grandad grows roses covered with greenfly. Which visitor would help the roses most? | Ladybirds | Ants | Goats | Hairy caterpillars
test: A child at a farm leaves a paper bag of crisps on a bench next to the goats. What may happen? | A goat may chew and swallow the bag | The goats will ignore it | The goats will guard it from other animals | The goats will be scared of it
test: Why is it unkind to stamp on beetles for fun? | Every small creature has a job in nature and can be hurt | Beetles will come back later to bite you in your sleep | Stamping makes your shoes smell | Beetles are protected only on Sundays
test: A friend says that if you chop a worm in two you get two worms. What should you say? | That is a myth; usually only one part survives, if any | That is true for all worms | That is true only for big worms | That is true if you wait a day
test: why we gotta shut the chooks in at night, they're fine | Foxes and other animals attack them in the dark | Chickens are afraid of the stars | So they lay their eggs only at night | Chickens get lost in the dark n cant find home
test: just collected the eggs, gonna have a biscuit | Wash your hands first, chickens can carry germs | Eggs are clean, no need to wash | Just blow on your hands first, that gets rid of germs | Only wash if an egg broke
test: sis says cut a worm in half n u get 2 worms. cool right | No, usually only one part survives, if any | Yes, each half grows into a whole worm | Yes, but only big worms do that | Yes, it makes two smaller worms
test: got head lice again, means we're dirty doesnt it | No, lice spread head to head and are common | Yes, lice only go to dirty hair | Yes, you must wash more often or they keep coming | Yes, only dirty children get lice
test: wanna kiss the baby chick, it's so fluffy | Do not kiss it and wash your hands after touching | Kiss it, chicks are clean | Kiss it if it is yellow and fluffy all over | Kiss it but not on the beak
test: goat's lowering its head at me | Move away calmly, it may butt you | Push its head down with both hands | Turn your back on it | Bend down to its level
test: Ruby says touching frogs and toads is totally fine. right? | No, wash your hands, some have poison or germs | Yes, they are always clean | Yes, they are tiny so they cannot hurt anyone at all | Yes, unless they are big
test: heard a fox got into the Lees' chicken coop and killed all of them cos they forgot to shut it | Real lesson: shut chickens in at night, because foxes and other predators attack in the dark | Real lesson: chickens should never be kept in gardens because foxes always get them in the end | It proves foxes only hunt in the day | It is gossip, so coops can be left open
test: everyone's saying Ella has nits cos her house is dirty | Wrong; head lice spread by head-to-head contact and are common, not a sign of being dirty | It is true, nits come from dirt, so if her house was clean she would never have caught them | It is true, if she has long hair | It is true, only messy kids get lice
test: someone's saying the new kid squashes ants for fun and is a psycho, should i pass it on | Do not pass it on; calling him names is cruel, and if it worries you, tell an adult | Pass it on, because it proves it | Pass it on, so people know to avoid him and keep away from him at break time | Pass it on, and add he hurts cats
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the wings of a butterfly? | They are fragile and covered with tiny scales which rub off when touched | They are strong and may be handled freely | They are composed of feathers, in the manner of a bird's, and are shed every year | They grow back immediately when damaged
exam: Identify the reason for which ants enter kitchens. | In search of food, wherefore crumbs and sticky spills should be cleaned | In search of cold water only, as ants require no food in houses whatsoever | Because they dislike the outdoors | Because they are attracted to light
exam: It being given that chickens or their eggs have been handled, select the practice consistent with established guidance. | The hands should be washed, chickens possibly carrying salmonella | The hands need no washing if the eggs appear clean | The eggs should be washed in the kitchen sink | The hands should be wiped on grass
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning goats. | Loose things should be kept out of reach, as goats chew almost anything | Goats eat only grass and ignore other things | Goats should be fed sweets as treats | Goats should be lifted by their horns
